{"hash":"04509f07c0b60265","voice":"en-IN-NeerjaExpressiveNeural","rate":"+4%","dur":85.542,"said":"Bol Bam! Come, walk with me down this narrow lane to Baba’s gate. Listen to the shopkeepers calling, smell the pera, Deoghar’s milk sweet, cooking in the stalls, and look at what leans against the walls: kanwars, bamboo yokes with a basket hanging from each end, dressed in saffron and hung with little bells. Every year, in the monsoon month of Shravan, pilgrims come down this lane in their lakhs with a kanwar on the shoulder. They have walked from Sultanganj on the Ganga, about a hundred and five kilometres away, most of them barefoot, carrying Ganga water filled at the start. By custom, that water is never set down on the ground along the way. They call one another ‘Bam’, and all night the road rings with ‘Bol Bam!’ Some, the Dak Bam, try to finish the whole walk within a single day. In 2025, about fifty-three lakh pilgrims came in that one month. Why carry water so far, to this one town? And why must it never touch the ground? Because of something that was set down here, long ago, and never lifted again. Come through the gate. The story begins with the king of Lanka.\n\nDid you know? At Kartik Purnima devotees bathe in Shivganga, the great tank near the temple, and set lamps afloat on its water.","segments":[{"key":"text","start":0,"end":1085},{"key":"didYouKnow","start":1101,"end":1214}],"words":[[0.098,0.349,0,3],[0.459,0.517,4,7],[1.827,0.445,9,13],[2.524,0.3,15,19],[2.837,0.192,20,24],[3.041,0.132,25,27],[3.185,0.264,28,32],[3.462,0.192,33,37],[3.666,0.337,38,44],[4.014,0.288,45,49],[4.315,0.108,50,52],[4.435,0.469,53,59],[4.916,0.397,60,64],[6.238,0.385,66,72],[6.635,0.096,73,75],[6.743,0.096,76,79],[6.851,0.709,80,91],[7.572,0.469,92,99],[8.245,0.337,101,106],[8.594,0.096,107,110],[8.702,0.505,111,115],[9.375,0.553,117,126],[9.94,0.216,127,131],[10.168,0.469,132,137],[10.841,0.325,139,146],[11.178,0.084,147,149],[11.274,0.084,150,153],[11.37,0.517,154,160],[12